## 1. The control added (new `selftest.sh:156–166`)

Appended immediately after the pre-existing post-end append control, following
the file's existing `run_case` / `check` conventions:

```bash
# Sealed-log integrity, byte view: the same post-end append WITHOUT a
# trailing newline must be caught too. A fresh sealed attempt is used so
# that the only difference from the control above is the missing LF: the
# 6 appended bytes form no new line, so the line count is unchanged and a
# line-view check sees nothing, while the byte-offset check sees exactly
# 6 bytes past the marker line.
run_case "plan-A no-LF append" "$PLAN_A" 0
latest_nolf=$(cat "$E/.latest-run")
printf 'abcdef' >>"$latest_nolf/harness.log"
bash "$E/check-log-integrity.sh" "$latest_nolf" >/dev/null 2>&1
check "post-end append without trailing newline detected" "$?" 1
```

Design notes, all mandated by attribution:

* **Fresh sealed attempt.** The pre-existing control tampers `$latest_a`
  *in place* with `"zombie writer append\n"`. Reusing that attempt would mean
  the no-LF control ran against an already-tampered log, so an rc of 1 could be
  attributed to the earlier LF append. `run_case "plan-A no-LF append"` creates a
  clean, normally sealed attempt first, so the 6 no-LF bytes are the *only*
  tamper in the log under test.
* **`printf 'abcdef'` with no `\n`** — exactly 6 bytes, matching the documented
  message shape `(6 byte(s) past the marker line, byte 2622 of 2628)`. Observed
  live: `(6 byte(s) past the marker line, byte 3215 of 3221)`.
* Assertion `check … "$?" 1`: the checker must exit non-zero. The `$?` is read
  immediately after the checker, no pipeline is involved.

## 2. Why the line-view checker misses it (mechanism, measured not assumed)

The sealed attempt's marker line is LF-terminated (in the final primary run it
ends `…509de352\n`). Appending
6 bytes without an LF leaves those bytes as an **unterminated extra line** that
the old checker's two primitives cannot see:

* `total=$(wc -l <"$LOG")` counts **newline characters**. The append adds none,
  so `total == last` and the guard `[ "$total" -gt "$last" ]` never fires.
* `recorded=$(sed -n "${last}p" "$LOG" …)` prints **only line `last`** — which is
  still exactly the marker line, because its own LF terminates it. The appended
  bytes live on unterminated line `last+1` and are never read.

Measured on the tampered log of the primary run
(`evidence/primary-07-oldchecker-internals.txt`): `last=36`, `total=36`
(`total -gt last` → no); `sed -n 36p` prints exactly 91 bytes — the marker line
alone, ending `…509de352\n`, with the 6 appended bytes absent from it — so
`recorded == prefix_sha` (both `d25b3cff…509de352`) and the old checker prints
`INTEGRITY OK`, while the byte view sees `wc -c = 3221` versus
`marker offset 3124 + 91 = 3215`. Add the LF and `wc -l` becomes 37 > 36, which the old
checker catches. The new checker never counts lines for this decision: it uses
`wc -c`, `grep -abo`, and `marker_end = marker_off + marker_line_bytes`
(`check-log-integrity.sh:34–49`), so 6 bytes past the marker is 6 bytes past.

## 3. Discriminating power

### 3.1 Real environment, one attempt, identical bytes (primary)

`work/proof-nolf.sh` (reproducible; scratch only) runs the frozen
`run-exclusive.sh` with `plan-a.txt` to get a genuinely sealed attempt, copies it,
appends the tamper, then runs **both** checkers on the **same directory** — no
copies between the two checker invocations, so the bytes are provably identical.
Raw rc values are captured with `$?` immediately after each command:

```
clean       : NEW rc=0  OLD rc=0
no-LF 6B    : NEW rc=1  OLD rc=0   <-- the control: NEW detects, OLD misses
6B + LF     : NEW rc=1  OLD rc=1   <-- identical payload, only the LF differs
```

Both checkers are read-only on the input: `harness.log` sha256 is identical
before the NEW run, between the runs, and after the OLD run
(`ae2cf07e187f9eb5…` in the final run; the geometry file prints all three).

Evidence: `evidence/primary-00…05*.log`, `evidence/primary-06-geometry.txt`.
Exit-code distinction is honoured throughout: "the command exited 0" is never
read as "the property holds" — an rc 0 from the OLD checker is reported as
*missed*, and `mirror-suite-*` rc 1 is reported as *environment*, not as a
checker verdict (see §5).

### 3.2 Suite level: same suite, old checker swapped in

Two complete mirrors were built under `dc-warn` with the correct layout
(`<mirror>/out/integration-a3-evidence/exclusive-runner`), differing **only** in
the checker bytes (verified: `606cb4f3…` vs `bebd0cf9…`). `run-exclusive.sh:46`
derives the attempt directory from `$ROOT`, so each mirror's `selftest.sh` also
has its hard-wired `ROOT=` line repointed to its own mirror root; apart from that
single line the mirror `selftest.sh` is byte-identical to the frozen one
(`diff` with the `ROOT=` value normalised → empty).

```
mirror-suite-new.log : rc 1, self-test: 26 pass, 9 fail
mirror-suite-old.log : rc 1, self-test: 25 pass, 10 fail
diff of the two logs : the ONLY behavioural difference is the new control
   line 36:  PASS post-end append without trailing newline detected      (NEW)
   line 36:  FAIL post-end append without trailing newline detected (got 0, want 1)  (OLD)
```

The 9 failures present in **both** arms are environmental (§5), and
`PASS post-end append detected` (the pre-existing LF control) passes in **both**
arms — so the old checker is not broken, it simply cannot see this case. This is
the suite-level proof that the new control pins the fix.

## 4. Attribution: the LF is the only variable

The no-LF and with-LF runs use the **same sealed attempt**, the same appended
payload bytes (`abcdef`), the same tamper command, and the same checker bytes.
Only the trailing LF differs, and it flips the OLD checker:

| | bytes | `wc -l` | delta vs clean | OLD rc |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| clean | 3215 | 36 | — | 0 (OK) |
| `abcdef` | 3221 | 36 | +6 bytes, **+0 lines** | **0 (MISSED)** |
| `abcdef\n` | 3222 | 37 | +7 bytes, **+1 line** | 1 (detected) |

So the miss is caused by the absence of the LF — not by a different payload, a
different tree, a different status file set, or a checker that failed to run
(the old checker printed a full `INTEGRITY OK … stage manifest exact` verdict,
i.e. it ran to completion and affirmatively reported the log as intact).

## 5. Limitations and honesty notes

* **The mirror runs are on `fuse.rclone`.** Confirmed: `findmnt -T dc-warn` →
  `fuse.rclone`; `chmod 755` returns rc 0 but the mode stays `644`, and `ln -s`
  fails with `EIO`. Consequently **both** mirror arms fail 9 checks for
  environmental reasons, identically: 2 symlink-dependent
  (`symlink attempt path rejected`, `plan-G internal symlink exit`) and 7
  option-C checks that need an executable wrapper (exit 126 and its dependents).
  This does **not** contaminate the differential — the arms differ in exactly one
  line — but it is why the mirror summaries are `26/9` and `25/10` rather than
  green. The primary §3.1 runs were done in the real (ext4) tree and suffer none
  of this.
* **`INVALID-suite-oldchecker-broken-env.log` is not evidence.** The first
  attempt at a mirror kept the frozen `ROOT`, so `run-exclusive.sh` wrote attempts
  to the frozen dir while the copy read `$E/.latest-run`; all paths collapsed to
  `/stages/*.status` and `/harness.log`. Its `14 pass, 21 fail` measures a broken
  environment, not the old checker. Kept and renamed as invalid; see
  `evidence/README.md`.
* **Frozen-artifact hygiene.** `check-log-integrity.sh` was never modified,
  overwritten, or reverted in place; the old checker was read from
  `dc-warn/out/integrity-bytefix/backup/check-log-integrity.sh.orig` and used only
  in scratch copies (hash of the backup unchanged: `bebd0cf9…`). Frozen-file
  hashes after the change: only `selftest.sh` moved (`961e1307…` → `81090001…`);
  `check-log-integrity.sh 606cb4f3…`, `run-exclusive.sh 7c332d33…`,
  `option-c-stage-wrapper.sh c14043c0…`, `option-c-poststate.py 4ad34edf…`,
  `option-c-child-records.py d1199267…`, `option-c.plan 42517f84…`,
  `README.md db36aaf8…` (mtime 03:07, untouched) are unchanged.
* The suite writes generated artifacts into the runner dir by design
  (`plan-*.txt`, `attempt-*`, `external-*`, `.latest-run`, `option-c-dummy`); the
  before/after runs did the same. No tracked source file besides `selftest.sh`
